---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const netCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The strength of weak ties', note: 'Granovetter (1973): acquaintances, not close friends, get you jobs. Close ties share your information; weak ties bridge to new pools. One of sociology\'s most cited results — 56,000+ citations and counting.' },
    { math: '2 · Small worlds and six degrees', note: 'Milgram\'s letter experiments (1967) → Watts & Strogatz (1998): a few random long-range links collapse network distance. "Six degrees" holds roughly — the structure, not friendliness, does it.' },
    { math: '3 · Social contagion', note: 'Obesity, smoking, happiness — behaviours and states cluster through networks (Christakis & Fowler), with identification debates (homophily vs contagion) that improved the field\'s causal toolkit (the statistics ladder\'s network problems).' },
    { math: '4 · Networks are inequality\'s plumbing', note: 'Jobs, information, norms flow through ties — so network segregation (who knows whom) reproduces inequality without any gatekeeper intending it. DiTomaso\'s "favoritism not racism" finding: whites get jobs through networks built by advantage, not exclusion intent.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/advanced/social-networks">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Social network analysis studies <strong>the structure of connections itself</strong> — not
    just who people are, but who they're linked to. Structure explains outcomes individuals
    can't: information flows, epidemics, opportunity hoarding, and the speed of everything
    social.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="weak ties, small worlds, contagion, structural inequality" widget="equationStep" config={netCfg} />

  <h2>Network thinking applied</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Disease through a network lens:</strong> superspreaders and degree distribution explain epidemic curves better than population averages — COVID made k-measures public vocabulary (the medicine ladder connects).</li>
    <li><strong>Online platforms are engineered networks:</strong> feeds rewire what connects to what — recommendation algorithms are network-structure machines, with echo-chamber effects real but smaller than headlines claim (the evidence is nuanced).</li>
    <li><strong>Social capital has forms:</strong> bonding (dense, supportive) vs bridging (spanning, opportunity-creating — Putnam) — healthy communities need both; each solves different problems.</li>
    <li><strong>Centrality measures translate to power:</strong> who sits between groups (brokers) captures disproportionate value — Burt's "structural holes": spanning disconnected clusters beats being popular inside one.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Granovetter, AJS (1973); Watts & Strogatz, Nature (1998); Christakis & Fowler, NEJM (2007); Burt, Structural Holes (1992); Putnam, Bowling Alone (2000).</p>
</TopicPage>
